    .title = Host the Tor SOCKS proxy listens on, usually 127.0.0.1
zen-tor-port-label = Proxy port
zen-tor-port-input =
    .title = Port the Tor SOCKS proxy listens on - 9050 for the system tor daemon, 9150 for Tor Browser
zen-tor-log =
    .label = Log proxy decisions to the console

## Bridges. Only reach our own tor (zen.tor.daemon.enabled) - there is no
## torrc to write bridge lines into for an external one.

zen-tor-bridges-header = Bridges
zen-tor-bridges-description = A bridge is an unlisted relay, for when the ordinary Tor network is itself blocked. Paste one bridge line per row, exactly as a bridge database or Tor Browser hands them out - "obfs4 …" and "snowflake …" lines both work.
zen-tor-bridges-enabled =
    .label = Use bridges
zen-tor-bridges-lines-input =
    .placeholder = obfs4 192.0.2.1:443 4142E3ED0A2E5AF0663E51A7593B6D82CC0A0E88 cert=… iat-mode=0
zen-tor-bridges-note = Bridges only apply to this browser's own tor, not an external daemon. A pluggable-transport line needs its binary on this machine (obfs4proxy, snowflake-client) - missing one stops this browser's tor from starting rather than connecting without it.
zen-tor-bridges-status-empty = No bridge lines yet.
zen-tor-bridges-status-ok =
    { $count ->
        [one] 1 bridge line ready.
       *[other] { $count } bridge lines ready.
    }
zen-tor-bridges-status-invalid =
    { $reason ->
        [empty] Only comments or blank lines - no bridge to use.
        [no-address] Line { $line }: no address:port.
        [obfs4-fingerprint] Line { $line }: obfs4 needs a 40-character fingerprint.
        [obfs4-cert] Line { $line }: obfs4 line is missing cert=.
        [snowflake-fingerprint] Line { $line }: snowflake line is missing a fingerprint.
       *[other] Line { $line } could not be parsed.
    }
zen-tor-bridges-transport-found = { $name }: found
zen-tor-bridges-transport-missing = { $name }: not installed

## The onion indicator. Three shapes, not three colours: the state has to be
## readable in a screenshot and without colour vision.

zen-tor-status-header = What the onion tells you
zen-tor-status-description = The onion in the toolbar says where this window's traffic is going. The shape carries the state and the colour only repeats it - purple for a Tor space, amber for a Tor window, red when something is wrong. It is an ordinary toolbar widget, so Customize Toolbar can move it or take it away.
zen-tor-status-off = Not routed
zen-tor-status-off-note = A dimmed outline onion with nothing behind it. Nothing in this window goes through Tor.
zen-tor-status-connecting = Connecting
zen-tor-status-connecting-note = Layers pulsing outward. Shown while a page is actually loading in a routed container, or while the proxy is still being checked - not a progress bar, since this browser has no Tor control port to ask.
zen-tor-status-connected = Routed
zen-tor-status-connected-note = A solid onion. The proxy answered a SOCKS5 greeting on the configured port and nothing is in flight.
zen-tor-status-unreachable = Unreachable
zen-tor-status-unreachable-note = A struck-through onion, in red. Either the setting above is off, or nothing is answering on the proxy port - pages in this space will fail to load rather than quietly going direct.

zen-tor-spaces-header = Spaces routed through Tor
zen-tor-spaces-description = Turning the setting above off only stops new Tor spaces being made. A space already on this list keeps refusing to load rather than falling back to a direct connection, so take it off the list here to get it browsing normally again.
zen-tor-spaces-empty = No space is routed through Tor.
zen-tor-space-unnamed = Container { $id }
zen-tor-space-unroute = Stop routing
    .title = Take this space off Tor and let it browse directly
