    .aria-label = Use the built-in mark again
zen-sites-icon-picker-title = Choose a site icon
zen-sites-icon-invalid = That icon was not accepted. It has to be an image file on this computer (.svg, .png, .gif, .jpg, .webp, .avif) or an inline data: image, and under 512 KB. Web addresses are refused on purpose.
zen-tor-harden =
    .label = Harden against fingerprinting while Tor spaces exist
zen-tor-harden-note = Gecko only offers this browser-wide, so it applies to every tab, not only Tor spaces. It turns on resist-fingerprinting and letterboxing — pages get padded with visible bars so the window size gives less away, and some sites will look or behave differently.

## Privacy pane. Every limit stated here is one that was read out of the Gecko
## source; docs/browser-safety.md records where. Nothing may be softened: a
## setting someone reads as stronger than it is does real harm.

pane-zen-privacy-title = Privacy
zen-privacy-title = Privacy

zen-privacy-lifetime-header = How long sites keep data
zen-privacy-lifetime-description = Cookies, logins and offline storage. Sites you sign in to should be set to keep — anything else signs you out.
zen-privacy-default-label = Sites without a rule
zen-privacy-lifetime-keep = Keep
zen-privacy-lifetime-session = Until { -brand-short-name } closes
zen-privacy-lifetime-tab = Until the last tab closes
zen-privacy-default-note-keep = Nothing is cleared automatically. Sites in the list below still follow their own rule.
zen-privacy-default-note-session = Every site is cleared when { -brand-short-name } closes, except the ones set to keep.
zen-privacy-default-note-tab = Every site is cleared when { -brand-short-name } closes, except the ones set to keep. Only sites set individually are cleared when their last tab closes.

zen-privacy-column-site = Site
zen-privacy-column-lifetime = Keeps data
zen-privacy-sites-empty = No site has a rule of its own.
zen-privacy-new-site =
    .placeholder = example.com
    .title = A site, without http:// — subdomains are covered too
zen-privacy-site-add = Add
zen-privacy-site-invalid = That is not a site address. Enter a domain such as example.com, without a scheme or a path.
zen-privacy-site-remove = Remove
    .title = Remove this rule; the site falls back to the default above
    .aria-label = Remove the rule for { $site }
zen-privacy-site-lifetime =
    .aria-label = How long { $site } keeps data
zen-privacy-lifetime-note = A rule covers the site and its subdomains, in every container. Data is only cleared once the site has no ordinary tab left anywhere — a private window neither triggers a clear nor prevents one, since private windows keep a separate copy of everything that is thrown away on their own.

zen-privacy-clear-header = What clearing removes
zen-privacy-clear-description = The same list applies whether a clear happens at shutdown or when a tab closes.
zen-privacy-clear-caches =
    .label = Also clear the site's caches
zen-privacy-clear-caches-note = A cached response identifies a returning visitor as well as a cookie does. Costs a re-download, nothing else.
zen-privacy-shutdown-cookies =
    .label = Clear cookies and site data when { -brand-short-name } closes
zen-privacy-shutdown-cache =
    .label = Clear caches when { -brand-short-name } closes
zen-privacy-shutdown-site-settings =
    .label = Clear site permissions when { -brand-short-name } closes
zen-privacy-shutdown-on = Clearing at shutdown is on. Sites set to keep are exempt — except from the cache row, which { -brand-short-name } can only apply to every site at once.
zen-privacy-shutdown-off = These three do nothing right now: clearing at shutdown is off, and it is the choice above that turns it on. Sites with a rule of their own are still cleared.
zen-privacy-clear-includes = Removed: cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, service workers, the Cache API, private file storage, HSTS state, media licences, reports and TLS session tickets.
zen-privacy-clear-leaves = Left behind: the DNS cache, the Alt-Svc cache, the OCSP cache and the HTTP authentication cache. Gecko cannot clear any of those for one site, so a cleared site can still be recognised by them until you restart. History, bookmarks, downloads and saved logins are never touched here.

zen-privacy-https-header = HTTPS
zen-privacy-https-description = What happens when a site is only reachable without encryption.
zen-privacy-https-label = When HTTPS fails
zen-privacy-https-first = Fall back quietly
zen-privacy-https-only = Refuse to load
zen-privacy-https-note-first = { -brand-short-name } tries HTTPS first and then loads the page over plain HTTP with no warning of any kind. This is Firefox's default and it is not a guarantee of anything.
zen-privacy-https-note-only = { -brand-short-name } stops and shows a warning page instead of loading anything unencrypted. Some older sites will not load at all.
zen-privacy-network-note = None of this hides which sites you visit from anyone watching your connection. Encrypted DNS, TLS 1.3 and Encrypted Client Hello still reveal the address you connect to — for about two thirds of addresses that is one site and no other — and the size and timing of the traffic identify the site on their own with better than 80% accuracy. Only Tor addresses an observer on the network.
zen-privacy-site-clear = Clear now
    .title = Clear this site's data now, without waiting for a tab to close
    .aria-label = Clear { $site }'s data now
zen-privacy-site-cleared = Cleared
    .aria-label = { $site }'s data cleared

# Agent control. The reasoning belongs in the copy: the trade is the point.
zen-agent-settings-header = Agent control
zen-agent-settings-enabled =
    .label = Let an assistant drive this browser
zen-agent-settings-description = Off by default. An assistant that drives a tab acts with your cookies and your sessions, so it can do anything you can. Turning this on creates a socket in your profile that only programs running as you can reach.
zen-agent-settings-isolate =
    .label = Give the agent's own tabs a separate container
zen-agent-settings-isolate-description = Keeps the agent's cookies apart from yours. Turning this off is what lets it act on sites you are signed in to, and is exactly what makes that dangerous.
zen-agent-settings-guarded =
    .label = Allow sign-in and payment sites
zen-agent-settings-guarded-description = Off by default. The protected list covers common sign-in and payment hosts; it cannot cover every bank.
zen-agent-settings-note = The socket lives in your profile and is readable only by programs running as you, so it is not a network service. That also means the token in it is not a defence against another program of yours — it stops an unrelated one connecting by accident. Every action, and every refusal, is written to a log you can read.

